--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Avatar (Implementation explanation)
Classes/modules created and their relationships
pepse.world.avatar.Avatar: a GameObject that encapsulates all player behavior:
input-driven horizontal movement, jump + double-jump rule,
energy consumption/regeneration, animation switching, and collision
handling with solid surfaces (ground + trunks).
pepse.world.avatar.AvatarState (enum): defines a finite set of avatar modes
 (IDLE, RUN, JUMP). The avatar updates its state each frame and uses it to decide
  which animation to render.
pepse.world.avatar.Energy: a UI element responsible only for
displaying the avatar’s current energy. It does not contain gameplay logic.
Relationships / coupling decisions:
PepseGameManager creates the Avatar and passes dependencies
(Dependency Injection) such as UserInputListener and ImageReader.
Energy receives a callback (avatar::getEnergy) rather than a concrete Avatar reference.
 This keeps the UI independent from the avatar’s internal implementation and reduces coupling.
How we designed state changes in the avatar
We implemented the avatar behavior as a finite-state machine using AvatarState.
Each frame (update), we compute the state based on grounded status and intended movement:
JUMP when not grounded
RUN when grounded and horizontal input produces non-zero velocity
IDLE when grounded and no horizontal input
When the state changes, the avatar switches its Renderable via updateAnimation()
 (state → correct AnimationRenderable). This centralizes visual behavior and
 prevents scattered conditionals.
How we designed energy changes, how it updates, and how we display it
Energy is encapsulated inside Avatar as a private field with controlled updates:
Idle regenerates energy up to MAX_ENERGY.
Run consumes energy while the avatar is actively moving.
Jump consumes a fixed cost; double-jump consumes a higher cost and is
allowed only under the project’s rule (while falling, once per airtime),
with a reset when grounded.
Energy is clamped to [0, MAX_ENERGY] to enforce an invariant
(defensive programming).
Display:
Energy.create(avatar::getEnergy) uses a Supplier/callback (Strategy-like design).
 The UI “asks” for the current energy each frame,
  so it always reflects the current value without the UI needing to know how energy is computed.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Trees / Flora (Implementation explanation)
Approach and main classes/modules
pepse.world.trees.Flora: a procedural generation module responsible for creating
all tree-related objects in a requested x-range. It does not add objects to the scene itself;
 it only produces GameObjects and returns them to the caller.
Tree parts are modeled as separate GameObjects:
Trunk blocks: implemented using pepse.world.Block with tag TRUNK_TAG.
Leaves: also implemented using Block with tag LEAF_TAG, plus lightweight animations (wind).
Fruits: implemented as a dedicated class pepse.world.trees.Fruit
(a GameObject) so it can encapsulate fruit-specific behavior (e.g., respawn timer / interaction),
 rather than overloading Block with extra responsibilities.
Design principles and relationships
Separation of concerns:
Flora only generates trees (pure “factory/creator” responsibility).
PepseGameManager / InfiniteWorldGenerator decide where and on which layer to
insert returned objects into the world.
Dependency Injection:
Flora receives Function<Float, Float> groundHeightFunc instead of depending directly on
 Terrain. This decouples tree placement from the terrain implementation and
 allows reuse/testing with any ground-height provider.
Deterministic generation:
For each snapped x-column, we seed a Random using hash(x, seed) so generation is repeatable.
 Same x-range + same seed → same trees.
generatedTreeXs prevents duplicate generation for already-generated columns.
forgetTreeX(int x) supports the infinite-world “prune and regenerate” mechanism
 by allowing a column to be generated again after removal.
How trees are built
Trunk:
Built as a vertical stack of Blocks aligned to the grid (Block.SIZE) from ground level upward.
Canopy (leaves + fruits):
Built in a square neighborhood around the trunk top (radius TREE_RADIUS),
 with probabilistic placement:
Leaves appear with LEAF_APPEARANCE_PROBABILITY
Fruits appear with FRUIT_APPEARANCE_PROBABILITY
Leaf animation:
Uses ScheduledTask to start animations after a random delay, then Transition for:
small angle oscillation (wind sway)
small width oscillation (wind “breathing” effect)
Integration with the world
InfiniteWorldGenerator / PepseGameManager take the produced objects and place them by tag:
trunk → Layer.STATIC_OBJECTS (solid)
leaf → Layer.STATIC_OBJECTS + 1 (drawn above trunk)
fruit → Layer.DEFAULT (interactive/collectible behavior if implemented)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Additional classes/packages we added (beyond the basic tasks)
pepse.world.avatar.Energy
Responsibility: UI element that displays the Avatar’s current energy level.
Design:
Uses a callback / functional interface approach: it receives something like
Supplier<Float> (in your code: avatar::getEnergy) so it pulls the latest value
 without the Avatar needing to know about UI.
This keeps low coupling between game logic (Avatar) and presentation (UI),
and follows Separation of Concerns.
Benefit: The Avatar remains focused on gameplay rules (movement, jump costs, regeneration),
 while Energy focuses only on rendering the current energy.
pepse.world.trees.Fruit
Responsibility: Represents a fruit as its own game object (not just a colored Block),
 so it can own fruit-specific behavior.
Design:
Encapsulates lifecycle behavior such as respawn timing (e.g., using ScheduledTask)
 and any future interactions (collection, collision response).
Keeps Flora as a generator rather than stuffing behavior into the generator.
Benefit: Applies Single Responsibility Principle—tree generation stays in Flora,
 fruit behavior stays in Fruit.
pepse.world.infinite package + InfiniteWorldGenerator
Responsibility: Implements the infinite world requirement by generating and removing
 terrain + flora around the Avatar as it moves.
Design:
Acts as an orchestration layer that coordinates between:
Terrain (ground blocks)
Flora (trees/leaves/fruits)
GameObjectCollection (adding/removing objects)
Uses state tracking (generatedMinX/generatedMaxX,
 and in your improved version spawnedByColumnX) to avoid duplicates and to support pruning.
Adds forgetColumn(x) / forgetTreeX(x) hooks to allow deterministic regeneration after pruning.
Benefit: Strong modularity—world expansion logic is isolated from PepseGameManager,
 keeping the manager simpler and making the infinite-world behavior easier to test and maintain.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
API changes (classes / methods / signatures)
Terrain API
Added: public void forgetColumn(int x)
Removes the snapped column x from the internal “already generated” set.
Purpose: lets the infinite-world system prune far-away terrain and still
 regenerate deterministically when the player returns
 (supports resource management + stateless regeneration idea on top of a stateful cache).
Flora API
Added: public void forgetTreeX(int x)
Removes the snapped column x from the internal “already generated” set.
Purpose: same idea as Terrain.forgetColumn, but for trees—enables pruning +
later regeneration while keeping Flora as the single owner of tree-generation bookkeeping.
Avatar API
Unchanged externally (constructor + getEnergy() stayed the same).
Internal behavior extension: collision handling was refined to distinguish:
“standing” collisions (top contact) vs.
“wall” collisions (side contact with trunks),
so movement/jumping rules remain correct without leaking this logic into other classes.
This keeps collision policy encapsulated inside Avatar (clients still just use
new Avatar(...) and getEnergy()).
InfiniteWorldGenerator (new API)
New class: pepse.world.infinite.InfiniteWorldGenerator
Key public method: public void update(float avatarX)
Called each frame by the game manager.
Encapsulates infinite-world behavior behind a minimal interface
(Manager delegates → Generator decides).
This is an abstraction boundary: the manager does not manage per-column spawning/removal details.